const cfg={"candidate":{"number":"292","id":"thomason-clifton-morality-meaning-god-2020","date":"2026-10-07","title":"SJ Thomason vs Scott Clifton — Morality, Meaning and God (2020)","label":"Morality and Meaning","topicCategory":"god-theism-atheism","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ycLfV4RVv5U","duration":"39 min","motion":"Do morality and meaning point to God?","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","sourceNote":"This was an informal conversation, with no formal opening rounds or closing speeches. At the editor’s request, this assessment covers only the direct philosophical exchanges at approximately 7:17–19:11, 22:27–36:37, and 1:02:32–1:15:37. Audience questions and all dependent answers and follow-ups, personal and political digressions, introductions and the outro are excluded. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the selected exchanges, not the complete recording. Source: complete public English auto-generated captions for the supplied January 17, 2020 recording, reviewed for source coverage and contextual speaker attribution. A brief speaker handoff near 31:48 was checked with a separate automated transcription of the audio; the following assent remains outside the scored argument. Captions contain recognition errors; quotations remain exact and source uncertainties are not scored as speaker defects. Summaries are condensed for clarity.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance, rather than judgments about worldview truth; the separate AI Contribution does not affect these scores.","sides":{"pro":{"name":"God grounds morality and higher purpose","speaker":"SJ Thomason","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Moral facts and meaningful lives do not require God","speaker":"Scott Clifton","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":70,"con":85,"winner":"con"},"summary":"Clifton’s careful separation of moral facts, duties, and finite meaning outperformed Thomason’s suggestive but under-supported appeals to transcendence and higher calling.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"we're all put here for a reason","context":"Thomason grants that atheists can construct satisfying everyday purposes, then states her stronger view that individual gifts belong within an assigned higher calling."},"con":{"text":"I think ultimately morality reduces to well-being","context":"Clifton distinguishes objective facts about conscious experience from intrinsic value and categorical obligation while explaining the positive basis of his secular moral account."}},"sections":[{"sectionId":"objective-moral-facts","title":"Objective moral facts and well-being","timebox":"8:16–25:36","score":{"pro":73,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-life-fairness-shared-values","time":"8:41","score":71,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason points to widespread concern for life and fair treatment as evidence that people recognize common objective values rather than merely reporting isolated personal preferences.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason begins with morally vivid common ground, identifying concern for life and fairness as recognizable human commitments that can anchor later discussion in concrete judgments rather than abstraction. Principal limitation: Widespread endorsement does not independently establish mind-independent value, and the broad language about what everyone accepts overlooks cultural variation, counterexamples, and naturalistic explanations of convergence. Live burden: Thomason must supply the missing inference from shared recognition to objective status and ultimately divine grounding; Clifton need only show that agreement remains compatible with his secular account. Locked score: 71 reflects a relevant and accessible opening premise with genuine dialectical usefulness, but the evidence is informal and the decisive agreement-to-objectivity bridge remains insufficiently defended.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-objective-wellbeing-facts","time":"8:16","score":82,"role":"Constructive","words":"Clifton compares morality with medicine: facts about harm and conscious well-being can remain objectively true even when attitudes differ, so secular objectivity need not collapse into relativism.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton establishes a concrete, intelligible secular conception of objectivity by comparing moral inquiry with medicine and invoking opinion-independent facts about poison, suffering, and conscious experience. Principal limitation: The analogy demonstrates that harms can be described objectively, but it compresses the further argument that moral wrongness should reduce specifically to well-being rather than another evaluative standard. Live burden: Clifton must explain why facts about conscious experience have moral relevance while preserving his explicit rejection of intrinsic value and categorical obligation, whereas Thomason must show why those facts require transcendence. Locked score: 82 reflects a strong foundational clarification whose examples and careful calibration substantially advance the secular case, while its central reduction remains announced more confidently than it is philosophically established.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-values-ground-right-and-wrong","time":"16:00","score":74,"role":"Reply","words":"Thomason applies life, liberty, and justice to rape, arguing that its violation of those values explains why the act is objectively wrong in every contemplated case.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason makes her values framework concrete through the specific rape example, showing how life, liberty, and justice can organize a morally serious judgment rather than remain abstract labels. Principal limitation: Agreement that rape violates those values does not establish their objectivity, completeness, or divine source, and the example does not discriminate her account from Clifton’s suffering-based explanation. Live burden: Thomason must defend the values’ status and grounding, then show how they generate duties; Clifton must explain whether well-being fully captures the wrong without assuming the intrinsic value he disputes. Locked score: 74 reflects clarity, relevance, and coherent application to the example, alongside limited evidential reach and an incomplete response to the competing secular standard within the precise exchange Clifton initiated.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-facts-values-duties-distinction","time":"9:04","score":88,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton separates objective well-being facts from intrinsic value and categorical duties, arguing that conditional reasons can guide action without importing an external cosmic obligation.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton precisely separates objective facts, well-being, intrinsic value, and categorical obligation, preventing apparent agreement about obvious harms from silently settling the stronger metaphysical and prescriptive questions. Principal limitation: His claim that morality reduces to well-being receives less defense than the distinctions themselves, and conditional reasons still require an account of why particular aims deserve practical authority. Live burden: Clifton must sustain a positive reasons-based account without accepting the categorical duties he rejects, while Thomason must argue rather than assume that objective moral facts entail intrinsic values or divinely grounded obligations. Locked score: 88 reflects exceptional conceptual control, responsiveness, and charitable calibration, with only the compressed defense of well-being keeping the move below a fully comprehensive resolution.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-reasons-not-intrinsic-properties","time":"25:12","score":77,"role":"Constructive","words":"Clifton denies that value or wrongness belongs intrinsically to acts or lives, proposing instead that moral status depends on reasons that can be objectively assessed.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton sharply distinguishes a reasons-based account from intrinsic-property language, preserving objective assessment while refusing to treat value as a mysterious feature residing inside actions or lives. Principal limitation: The move largely states rather than defends its theory, leaving unspecified what makes a reason morally relevant, how reasons are weighed, and what authority they possess for indifferent agents. Live burden: Clifton must connect assessable reasons to his well-being framework without smuggling in intrinsic value, while Thomason must show why intrinsic or divinely grounded value better explains the same judgments. Locked score: 77 reflects a useful and clearly formulated clarification with genuine burden relevance, constrained by sparse illustration, speculative attribution of agreement to Thomason, and an undeveloped account of reasons.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"transcendent-source-and-duties","title":"Transcendent grounding and categorical duties","timebox":"14:33–17:41","score":{"pro":70,"con":87},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-transcendent-source-across-eras","time":"14:33","score":70,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason argues that morality extending across peoples, generations, and eras should have a correspondingly transcendent source rather than rest on changing human opinions.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason directly addresses the motion with a memorable source-matching inference: morality with transhistorical reach is better explained by a ground that likewise transcends particular societies and eras. Principal limitation: Temporal or cultural stability does not itself require a personal transcendent source, and the argument leaves stable natural facts, facts about consciousness, and other human-independent alternatives substantially unexamined. Live burden: Thomason must justify why a truth’s scope determines the metaphysical kind of its source and why that source is divine; Clifton’s burden is to clarify relevant secular possibilities when advancing his own account, not to disprove her premise by completing one. Locked score: 70 reflects strong relevance and clear direction, constrained by weak comparative warrant and underdeveloped elimination of rival explanations.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-competing-objective-standards","time":"15:05","score":84,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton contrasts wrongness as conflict with God’s nature against wrongness as unnecessary suffering, showing that objectivity alone cannot select between competing proposed standards.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton uses a disciplined parallel comparison to expose underdetermination: both divine-nature and well-being accounts can formulate objective claims, so objectivity cannot uniquely identify the theistic standard. Principal limitation: Presenting two coherent standards does not establish that the well-being standard is correct, and neither standard receives an independent foundation within this particular reply. Live burden: Clifton must explain why unnecessary suffering supplies the preferable moral criterion, while Thomason must give reasons that divine nature is more than another stipulated definition of wrongness within this direct comparison. Locked score: 84 reflects a highly responsive and precise challenge that materially weakens the inference from objectivity to God, while appropriately stopping short of claiming that the comparison completes the secular theory.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-fact-does-not-entail-obligation","time":"16:44","score":89,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton distinguishes the proposition that rape is wrong from the proposition that one ought not rape, arguing that an additional premise is required to derive obligation.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton isolates two distinct propositions with unusual precision, demonstrating that a description of moral status and a prescription governing action cannot simply be treated as synonymous without further argument. Principal limitation: The conceptual distinction alone does not refute every theory that derives obligations from sufficiently authoritative reasons, interests, or relationships, and those rival bridges are not evaluated here. Live burden: Clifton must explain his conditional practical reasons after denying categorical obligation, while Thomason must identify and defend the premise connecting objective wrongness with what agents are bound to do. Locked score: 89 reflects the exchange’s strongest targeted clarification, combining logical coherence, direct responsiveness, terminological stability, and appropriate modesty about what the distinction proves.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"motivation-and-divine-command","title":"Moral motivation and divine command","timebox":"23:30–31:53","score":{"pro":67,"con":86},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-common-values-transcendent-lord","time":"23:30","score":67,"role":"Reply","words":"Thomason says the central disagreement concerns whether shared objective values arise from consequences and desire or instead receive their source from a transcendent Lord.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason clearly restates the motion-level disagreement and identifies divine sourcehood as the feature intended to distinguish her moral framework from Clifton’s secular alternative. Principal limitation: The move repeats the transcendent conclusion without supplying a new bridge, and it mischaracterizes Clifton’s view as grounded in desire despite his explicit distinction between desires and objective well-being facts. Live burden: Thomason must accurately engage Clifton’s actual position and demonstrate why common values favor a transcendent Lord; Clifton must clarify his positive account without treating correction alone as proof at this point in their direct exchange. Locked score: 67 reflects substantial relevance and an intelligible contrast, offset by weak supporting warrant, reduced responsiveness, and an uncharitable compression of the opposing account.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-conditional-ought-without-cosmic-binding","time":"24:03","score":87,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton argues that conditional oughts can follow from aims such as being moral, while objective well-being remains independent of desire and requires no cosmic external enforcement.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton corrects the desire attribution and supplies a positive practical model in which facts about well-being combine with adopted aims to generate reasons for helping others without cosmic compulsion. Principal limitation: The example assumes an aim of being moral and does not fully explain why an agent should adopt that aim, how competing aims are ranked, or why maximizing well-being is authoritative. Live burden: Clifton must show that conditional reasons suffice for the practical work under discussion; Thomason must establish that categorical divine binding supplies additional truth or guidance rather than merely stronger motivational sanctions in Clifton’s own example. Locked score: 87 reflects an exceptionally responsive, clear, and carefully limited reply whose remaining weakness lies in the foundation of the aims and evaluative standard it employs.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-divine-sanction-shares-motivation-limit","time":"28:19","score":85,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton argues that divine sanctions cannot motivate someone indifferent to hell, so categorical obligation adds no practical leverage when the wrongdoer rejects the relevant values.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton constructs a forceful symmetry challenge: secular and theistic appeals alike fail to motivate an agent who rejects every relevant value, including concern about punishment or hell. Principal limitation: Motivational effectiveness and normative truth are different questions, so demonstrating that a sanction cannot move an indifferent wrongdoer does not establish that categorical obligation lacks reality or authority. Live burden: Clifton must limit the conclusion to the alleged practical advantage of divine duties; Thomason must explain whether her grounding claim concerns truth, accountability, motivation, or some defensible combination of them in this exchange. Locked score: 85 reflects a vivid, coherent, and highly responsive rebuttal that defeats a common motivational claim, while its reach remains narrower than a metaphysical refutation of obligation.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-contrary-consequences-divine-command-test","time":"30:26","score":85,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton stipulates divine commands producing universal misery while forbidden acts produce flourishing, testing whether moral intuition tracks bare obligation or consequences for conscious beings.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton’s carefully controlled hypothetical grants certainty about God and reverses every welfare consequence, isolating whether moral judgment tracks divine obligation or the condition of conscious beings. Principal limitation: An extreme hypothetical can expose conflicting commitments without establishing a complete moral theory, and a sophisticated divine-command account may deny that a perfectly good God could command the stipulated misery. Live burden: Clifton must explain why the intuitive verdict identifies well-being as the controlling standard; if Thomason rejects the scenario, she must show why that response preserves rather than assumes the connection between divine goodness and welfare. Locked score: 85 reflects strong coherence, precision, responsiveness, and charitable pressure, limited by the contestable modal stipulation and by the inability of one intuition pump to establish the full secular account.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"cross-cultural-evidence","title":"Cross-cultural agreement and naturalistic explanation","timebox":"11:43–36:37","score":{"pro":72,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-moral-foundations-common-values","time":"11:43","score":74,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason reports Haidt’s survey and cross-disciplinary research as finding recurring foundations such as care and fairness, offering empirical support for cross-human moral commonality.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason introduces a named research program, a substantial reported sample, and differentiated moral foundations, giving her commonality claim more empirical specificity than an appeal to intuition alone. Principal limitation: The conversational report supplies neither methodological detail nor evidence that recurring psychological foundations are objective norms, and it does not compare divine grounding with evolutionary or social explanations. Live burden: Thomason must distinguish descriptive recurrence from normative truth and then connect that truth to God; Clifton must explain why the same pattern can arise naturally without treating possibility as disproof. Locked score: 74 reflects useful, relevant empirical material and generally clear exposition, tempered by overbroad universality language and an incomplete movement from shared psychology to metaphysical morality.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-golden-rule-descriptive-cooperation","time":"18:20","score":83,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton explains the Golden Rule as a wise cooperative principle for evolved social beings, separating its practical benefits from claims of categorical or divine authority.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton preserves the Golden Rule’s practical importance while giving its recurrence a specific secular explanation through social dependence, fairness, cooperation, and the benefits of avoiding reciprocal exploitation. Principal limitation: The evolutionary and social genealogy is plausible but only sketched, and explaining why communities endorse a norm does not independently determine whether the norm also has objective or divine grounding. Live burden: Clifton must keep his conclusion at compatibility with naturalism and articulate how conditional reasons retain normative traction; Thomason must show what explanatory work transcendence adds beyond the cooperative account. Locked score: 83 reflects a strong, charitable, and directly responsive alternative explanation, with evidential incompleteness preventing the genealogy from becoming a decisive metaphysical conclusion.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-golden-rule-cross-cultural-duty","time":"17:40","score":71,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason treats the Golden Rule’s recurrence across religions, cultures, and humanist commitments as evidence for a universal prescriptive duty rather than a local convention.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason offers a familiar, intelligible candidate for universal duty and connects the abstract is-ought discussion to a norm whose cross-cultural recurrence can be investigated empirically. Principal limitation: Descriptive prevalence does not generate categorical prescription without an additional normative premise, and the universal wording receives no documentation, treatment of variant formulations, or analysis of counterexamples. Live burden: Thomason must show why recurring endorsement tracks truth rather than cooperation, inheritance, or convergent psychology; Clifton must preserve the rule’s practical wisdom while explaining why that usefulness need not imply divine authority. Locked score: 71 reflects a pertinent and clearly expressed contribution whose main evidential observation is meaningful, but whose decisive descriptive-to-prescriptive inference remains unsupported.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-agreement-does-not-prove-metaphysics","time":"34:42","score":87,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton argues that shared intuitions can arise through evolution and social selection, so psychological convergence cannot by itself establish objective morality or a divine metaphysical source.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton identifies the precise category shift from psychological commonality to metaphysical truth and supplies a coherent rival genealogy involving social cooperation, selection, and evolved moral intuitions. Principal limitation: The naturalistic genealogy remains a plausible explanatory possibility rather than a demonstrated history, so it undercuts Thomason’s inference without independently proving that objective morality or God is absent. Live burden: Clifton must preserve that calibrated underdetermination claim, while Thomason must provide evidence that distinguishes transcendent grounding from a psychology-producing natural process. Locked score: 87 reflects outstanding responsiveness, conceptual precision, burden relevance, and charity, supported by a specific alternative explanation whose empirical development remains the principal limitation.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-cross-cultural-findings-to-objective-duties","time":"32:07","score":72,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason proposes cross-cultural convergence in is-ought judgments as a method for supporting universal duties, asking whether independently recurring norms warrant calling them objective.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason explicitly formulates the methodology she wants assessed, connecting empirical convergence with the search for universal duties and presenting the bridge as a question open to scrutiny. Principal limitation: Even robust agreement would establish common judgment more directly than mind-independent obligation, and the proposal gives no criteria for sampling independence, evidential strength, or excluding shared causal influences. Live burden: Thomason must add a defensible inference from convergence to normative truth; Clifton must explain underdetermination without assuming that a naturalistic possibility automatically defeats objectivity across the cultures and eras she invokes. Locked score: 72 reflects central relevance, clarity, and constructive engagement, balanced against a substantial descriptive-to-normative gap and limited methodological specification.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-humane-orientation-commonality","time":"33:35","score":73,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason reports cross-country research finding aspirations for greater humane orientation than current practice, using that repeated is-ought pattern as evidence of common norms.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason provides a concrete practice-versus-aspiration pattern from a named cross-cultural study, giving the proposed convergence method a specific empirical application rather than leaving it hypothetical. Principal limitation: The study description omits design details and establishes shared aspirations more readily than objective duties, while the added suggestion that suffering may reflect divine planning substantially exceeds the reported evidence. Live burden: Thomason must separate the empirical commonality from her theological interpretation and justify each inferential step; Clifton must address the pattern without treating evolutionary explanation as already demonstrated. Locked score: 73 reflects relevant evidence, understandable exposition, and useful specificity, offset by incomplete methodological support, metaphysical underdetermination, and an especially poorly calibrated theological gloss.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"higher-and-finite-purpose","title":"Higher purpose and finite meaning","timebox":"1:02:49–1:08:14","score":{"pro":72,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-higher-god-given-purpose","time":"1:02:49","score":85,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason explicitly grants atheists work, family, and everyday aims while reserving a distinct higher category for a God-given calling that secular life lacks.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason carefully narrows her claim, expressly granting ordinary atheist purposes and distinguishing them from the higher creator-assigned calling she intends to defend, thereby avoiding a common caricature. Principal limitation: The move defines the proposed category much more successfully than it demonstrates that such a category exists, confers additional meaning, or is unavailable through secular ideals. Live burden: Thomason must justify both the reality of a divine calling and the added value supplied by assignment; Clifton must address that qualified proposal while explaining why finite, self-directed purposes need no higher authorization. Locked score: 85 reflects exceptional clarity, responsiveness, relevance, and charitable calibration as a scope-setting move, with the limited evidential support appropriate to a careful clarification rather than a completed argument.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-no-external-purpose-no-internal-problem","time":"1:03:24","score":84,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton accepts that atheism lacks creator-assigned teleology but denies that this creates an internal contradiction or prevents a life from being meaningful on secular terms.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton openly accepts the exact consequence Thomason identifies and then asks for the missing premise that would turn absence of creator-assigned purpose into an internal defect or meaningfulness failure. Principal limitation: First-person acceptance establishes consistency more readily than a general positive theory of meaning, and the move does not yet specify which relationships, projects, or experiences make finite lives valuable. Live burden: Clifton must develop the finite account beyond simply denying a problem; Thomason must show why externally assigned purpose is necessary or superior rather than merely unavailable on atheism in the creator-assignment sense Thomason specifies. Locked score: 84 reflects a coherent, direct, and admirably calibrated response that shifts attention to the disputed consequence, while leaving fuller constructive support to subsequent moves.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-gifts-point-to-assigned-calling","time":"1:04:03","score":72,"role":"Reply","words":"Thomason argues that universal meaning-seeking and varied personal gifts fit the view that people were placed here for reasons discoverable through their individual callings.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason gives positive content to higher purpose by connecting human meaning-seeking, distinctive abilities, development, and vocation within an intelligible picture of an assigned calling. Principal limitation: Those features are equally compatible with evolved psychology, social formation, and self-constructed meaning, while the unspecified appeal to studies does not discriminate among these explanations. Live burden: Thomason must show why gifts and aspirations are evidence of divine assignment rather than merely compatible with it; Clifton may withhold that inference by demonstrating the explanatory underdetermination without supplying a complete secular origin story. Locked score: 72 reflects an engaging and relevant constructive picture with clear practical significance, tempered by weak comparative warrant and an inference from compatibility with theism to evidential support for theism.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-finitude-does-not-remove-value","time":"1:05:59","score":83,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton uses a finite movie’s narrative value to argue that endings and eventual forgetting need not erase meaning and may contribute to intelligible resolution.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton’s movie analogy supplies an accessible counterexample to the alleged necessity of endless duration, showing how finitude, structure, and resolution can coexist with genuine experiential and narrative value. Principal limitation: The analogy establishes compatibility rather than a comprehensive account of meaningful life, and attributing the concern to ego introduces unnecessary motive speculation that weakens an otherwise substantive reply. Live burden: Clifton must identify what grounds the value of finite projects and relationships beyond the analogy; Thomason must show why permanence is necessary for meaning rather than simply asserting that eventual disappearance cancels present goods. Locked score: 83 reflects a coherent, vivid, and directly responsive rebuttal with high burden relevance, moderated by its limited positive theory and avoidable uncharitable rhetoric.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-atheism-ends-in-forgetting","time":"1:05:27","score":67,"role":"Reply","words":"Thomason argues that cosmic accident, finitude, and eventual forgetting deprive atheistic life of ultimate meaning, making theism or deism attractive as a nontransient framework.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason squarely addresses ultimate meaning and offers a psychologically intelligible explanation of why cosmic contingency, mortality, and eventual forgetting can motivate inquiry into theism or deism. Principal limitation: The conclusion depends on an undefended criterion equating meaning with permanence or remembrance, shifts among several senses of significance, and does not answer the finite purposes she previously granted. Live burden: Thomason must show why transient experiences and relationships cannot possess sufficient value; Clifton must provide a positive finite alternative rather than merely rejecting emotional discomfort. Locked score: 67 reflects strong responsiveness and motion-level relevance, but weak logical and evidential support, imprecise movement among concepts, and overstatement of atheism’s purported conclusion substantially reduce its force.","tags":[{"type":"fallacy","label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/","context":"After granting atheists everyday purposes, Thomason treats cosmic accident and eventual forgetting as showing that atheist life has no meaning."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-god-purpose-regress-challenge","time":"1:07:10","score":86,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton applies the need for externally assigned purpose to uncreated God, arguing that the criterion creates an unexplained asymmetry if divine existence remains meaningful without assignment.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton turns Thomason’s proposed necessary condition back onto uncreated God, exposing a focused asymmetry and inviting a principled distinction rather than assuming that theism is incoherent. Principal limitation: The objection remains diagnostic because Thomason may distinguish necessary divine being from created agency, and the move does not preclude accounts on which God’s nature contains intrinsic purpose. Live burden: Clifton must show that the features relevant to purpose are genuinely parallel across God and humans; Thomason must explain why necessary being provides intrinsic purpose while created agency requires an externally assigned end. Locked score: 86 reflects excellent coherence, precision, responsiveness, and calibrated questioning, supported by strong internal warrant even though a developed necessary-being distinction could potentially answer the challenge.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-divine-being-and-accountability","time":"1:07:48","score":62,"role":"Reply","words":"Thomason distinguishes God’s uncreated, unchanging being from created activity and adds that belief in God supplies humans with a source of external accountability.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason recognizes the need for a relevant difference and invokes God’s uncreated mode of being, while connecting the theistic framework to a concrete human function through external accountability. Principal limitation: Naming a being-doing distinction does not explain why unassigned divine existence is meaningful while human meaning requires assignment, and the pivot to reasons for belief leaves God’s own purpose unanswered. Live burden: Thomason must develop a principled metaphysical response to the asymmetry; Clifton need only maintain the challenge until that distinction is explained rather than redirecting the exchange toward human accountability. Locked score: 62 reflects a relevant attempted reply with some practical connection, but weak warrant, conceptual ambiguity, and limited responsiveness leave the central regress-style objection substantially unresolved.","tags":[{"type":"fallacy","label":"Red herring","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/","context":"Asked why uncreated God is not purposeless, Thomason turns from God’s purpose to human belief and external accountability."}]}}]},{"sectionId":"christian-moral-coherence","title":"Christianity, salvation, and eternal life","timebox":"1:10:01–1:15:37","score":{"pro":68,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-jesus-love-mercy-and-justice","time":"1:12:16","score":70,"role":"Reply","words":"Thomason presents Jesus as embodied love and substitutionary atonement as reconciling divine mercy with justice, thereby offering forgiveness without abandoning accountability for wrongdoing.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason supplies positive Christian content through an intelligible structure connecting love, moral example, mercy, justice, punishment, forgiveness, and reconciliation rather than relying on generic divine authority. Principal limitation: The account assumes that transferring punishment resolves the fairness problem, does not defend that assumption, and leaves Clifton’s specific concern about default damnation and belief-conditioned rescue substantially unanswered. Live burden: Thomason must explain the moral legitimacy and relevance of substitution to the challenged salvation conditions; Clifton must engage the strongest reconciliation offered rather than dismissing its language alone. Locked score: 70 reflects considerable relevance and understandable theological structure, offset by modest evidence, incomplete responsiveness, and insufficient acknowledgment of the precise objection already on the table.","tags":[{"type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/","context":"Thomason presents Jesus taking human punishment as already reconciling God’s perfect mercy with perfect justice."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-convinced-yet-moral-questions-remain","time":"1:10:01","score":86,"role":"Constructive","words":"Clifton accepts the hypothetical’s stipulated conviction, grants a morally perfect being in principle, but separates divine existence from the authority of every fallible biblical portrayal.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton honors the hypothetical’s eventual-conviction stipulation, explicitly accepts a morally perfect being in principle, and carefully distinguishes that concession from endorsing every scriptural portrayal or doctrine. Principal limitation: Fallible human authorship is a plausible reconciliation but remains only briefly supported, and the move does not identify a complete method for separating accurate revelation from cultural projection. Live burden: Clifton must justify particular moral criticisms without converting them into rejection of every possible God; Thomason must connect the stipulated appearance to specific biblical authority rather than treating generic conviction as doctrinal validation. Locked score: 86 reflects strong coherence, responsiveness, precision, and exceptional charity, with the evidential basis for the proposed authorship explanation remaining incomplete.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-heaven-is-not-earth-forever","time":"1:13:19","score":64,"role":"Constructive","words":"Thomason suggests that some objections target a mistaken picture of heaven as endless ordinary earthly life, so a more accurate conception might alter its desirability.","critique":"Strongest feature: Thomason identifies a legitimate interpretive possibility: objections to an everlasting afterlife may lose force if they depend on treating heaven as an indefinitely prolonged version of ordinary earthly experience. Principal limitation: She offers no positive alternative conception, generalizes about atheist and agnostic expectations, and tentatively attributes to Clifton a weaker boredom objection that he immediately disavows. Live burden: Thomason must explain how endless heavenly consciousness answers concerns about psychological change, personal identity, and freedom; Clifton must show why those problems persist across every coherent form of eternal conscious existence. Locked score: 64 reflects a relevant but thin anticipatory distinction whose clarity cannot compensate for sparse warrant, reduced responsiveness, imprecise targeting, and comparatively weak charitable engagement.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-salvation-system-moral-objection","time":"1:11:15","score":79,"role":"Constructive","words":"Clifton argues that default damnation and rescue conditioned on belief and credit appear incompatible with moral perfection, even while leaving perfect divinity possible in principle.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton reconstructs a concrete salvation arrangement and identifies a specific moral tension between perfect benevolence and rescue withheld unless the endangered person acknowledges and credits the rescuer. Principal limitation: The objection depends on a contested theological model, receives no scriptural or denominational substantiation here, and does not consider alternative Christian accounts of judgment, grace, or postmortem destiny. Live burden: Clifton must show that the criticized structure belongs to Thomason’s defended framework; Thomason must answer its conditional-rescue concern rather than only presenting atonement generally. Locked score: 79 reflects a coherent, relevant, and clearly expressed subsidiary challenge with strong responsiveness, moderated by limited warrant and an overbroad treatment of one disputed salvation formulation.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-atonement-extortion-objection","time":"1:13:34","score":83,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton grants the attractiveness of Jesus-as-love in principle but uses a parent analogy to argue that the specific salvation arrangement still resembles coercive extortion.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton explicitly acknowledges the appeal of Thomason’s love principle before distinguishing it from the concrete rescue conditions, ensuring that his criticism targets implementation rather than simply rejecting Christian vocabulary. Principal limitation: The parent-and-jail analogy depends on disputed similarities between human parenting and divine judgment, and the rhetorically forceful extortion label receives less argument than the underlying conditional-rescue concern. Live burden: Clifton must defend the analogy’s relevant parallels; Thomason must reconcile the actual conditions with the love and justice principles she invokes. Locked score: 83 reflects a highly responsive, clear, and substantially charitable reply with strong coherence, while the contested theological reconstruction and compressed analogy limit its evidential conclusiveness.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-eternal-heaven-identity-objection","time":"1:14:15","score":81,"role":"Reply","words":"Clifton argues that endless consciousness becomes torment, while psychological alteration sufficient to prevent it threatens personal identity and creates a further problem for freedom.","critique":"Strongest feature: Clifton corrects the earth-forever framing and develops a connected dilemma involving endless experience, eventual satiation, psychological transformation, continued personal identity, and the status of free will. Principal limitation: The universal claim that every never-ending conscious state becomes torment rests largely on intuition, and the argument assumes a comparatively fixed experiential structure that alternative accounts of heaven may deny. Live burden: Clifton must defend those experiential and identity premises; Thomason must offer a coherent eternal model that preserves the person rather than merely asserting that heaven differs from earth. Locked score: 81 reflects strong responsiveness, conceptual ambition, and clear argumentative progression, tempered by contestable universal premises and limited evidence for the central psychological prediction.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":70,"strengths":["She explicitly preserved ordinary atheist purposes while defining her narrower claim about a higher God-given calling.","She introduced identifiable cross-cultural research and concrete moral examples instead of relying exclusively on abstract theological assertion.","She supplied positive Christian content connecting Jesus, love, mercy, justice, accountability, and reconciliation."],"blunders":[{"text":"She repeatedly moved from widespread moral agreement to objective or divine grounding without defending the necessary inferential bridge.","links":[]},{"text":"Her reply to Clifton’s God-purpose challenge named a distinction but shifted toward human accountability without resolving the asymmetry.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":85,"strengths":["He consistently separated objective well-being facts, intrinsic value, conditional reasons, and categorical obligations with unusual precision.","He offered naturalistic alternatives while carefully limiting them to underdetermining theistic conclusions rather than disproving God.","He directly answered the permanence argument with finite meaning and exposed the external-purpose criterion’s pressure on uncreated God."],"blunders":[{"text":"His claim that morality reduces to well-being remained considerably less defended than his effective conceptual distinctions.","links":[]},{"text":"His ego attribution and universal claim that endless consciousness becomes torment were rhetorically stronger than their support.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"Convergent moral experience and purposive human capacities are jointly more expected if persons and their ends have a personal transcendent ground.","premises":["Humans across diverse settings recurrently recognize reasons concerning care, fairness, dignity, and accountability despite substantial disagreement about application.","Those recurring reasons are experienced as standards capable of criticizing both individual desire and prevailing social practice.","Human beings also characteristically interpret distinctive abilities and responsibilities through narratives of vocation that reach beyond immediate preference satisfaction.","A perfectly good personal ground would jointly explain why normative reasons appear authoritative and why rational agents experience capacities as answerable to higher purposes.","The combined explanatory fit is stronger than either cultural convergence or meaning-seeking considered independently, provided rival naturalistic explanations remain comparatively less unified."],"conclusion":"Therefore moral convergence and vocation can provide cumulative, defeasible support for God when the personal-ground hypothesis explains their authority and unity better than specified secular rivals."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Normative authority as relationship","text":"Moral authority can arise from accountability-generating relationships between rational creatures and a perfectly good creator, rather than from an unexplained source that merely matches morality’s temporal reach. Duties would then reflect relations to the giver of human capacities, to other persons, and to the goods those capacities enable. This supplies a relational premise in answer to Clifton’s fact-duty distinction, but it still requires reasons that creation generates legitimate authority and that goodness constrains divine commands rather than merely following them."},{"title":"Vocation through responsible fit","text":"Distinctive abilities count as evidence of assigned purpose only when they align with responsibilities, durable fulfillment, and needs those abilities are unusually suited to meet. A calling would then integrate capacity, obligation, and service rather than function as a pleasant self-description. The inference remains abductive rather than deductive because natural selection and social learning can also produce meaningful fit, but the relevant evidence would be the structured conjunction of ability and responsibility instead of the mere existence of talents or a widespread desire for meaning."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"Objective welfare facts can ground interpersonal reasons, and finite lives can possess meaning, without intrinsic value properties or externally imposed categorical purpose.","premises":["Facts about conscious suffering, flourishing, agency, and social dependence can be investigated independently of any person’s immediate preferences.","Practical reasons arise conditionally from defensible aims and relationships, without adding a separate cosmic property of categorical obligatoriness.","Cross-cultural convergence is evidentially compatible with shared psychology, evolutionary pressures, and recurring conditions of cooperative social life.","Finite experiences and projects can possess narrative, relational, and experiential value even when they end and are eventually forgotten.","Externally assigned purpose cannot be necessary for meaningful existence if an uncreated God may be meaningful without receiving such an assignment."],"conclusion":"Therefore the arguments offered do not establish that moral objectivity or meaningful life requires God, while a secular account can preserve truth, reasons, and finite significance."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Public reasons constraint","text":"Moral reasons gain interpersonal force when they are publicly assessable through effects on conscious agents, reciprocity, and agency, and when no affected person could reasonably claim an asymmetric exemption from the same standard. Their authority need not depend merely on whatever goals an individual happens to hold. This does not create the external categorical property Clifton rejects, but it answers the concern that an agent escapes morality by announcing indifference: the assessment concerns justifiability among affected persons rather than the agent’s motivational enthusiasm."},{"title":"Finite value without remembrance","text":"Finite experiences and relationships can retain value through their causal contributions: they improve lives, shape later choices, or realize worthwhile capacities while they exist. Permanent remembrance is unnecessary when completed benefits and relationships contain their value without an eternal observer’s record. Endings do not automatically create value, and some finite lives remain tragic or deprived; the narrower conclusion is that finitude alone neither erases every good a life contains nor creates a need for an externally assigned cosmic narrative."}]}}},"origin":"http://127.0.0.1:4176","prefix":"debate-292-local","screenshots":true,"screenshotDirectory":"output/playwright","requiredReaderDisclosure":"This was an informal conversation, with no formal opening rounds or closing speeches. At the editor’s request, this assessment covers only the direct philosophical exchanges at approximately 7:17–19:11, 22:27–36:37, and 1:02:32–1:15:37. Audience questions and all dependent answers and follow-ups, personal and political digressions, introductions and the outro are excluded. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the selected exchanges, not the complete recording.","scopeCases":[{"id":"licona-carrier-resurrection-formal-rounds-2004","number":"255","notice":"Only the formal debate rounds (5:10–1:38:23) are assessed. The subsequent question-and-answer session, including moderator-authored challenges and both speakers' replies, is excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"aron-ra-jones-christianity-dangerous-formal-rounds-2019","number":"256","notice":"Only the opening statements and cross-examination (1:59–1:27:22) are assessed. All subsequent audience questions and answers, and both speakers' later closing statements, are excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"atkins-law-science-philosophy-formal-rounds-2014","number":"257","notice":"Only the opening statements and first rebuttals (3:01–28:50) are assessed. The entire subsequent audience-question portion, including both speakers' later replies, concessions, and concluding comments, is excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"white-price-bible-truth-formal-rounds-2010","number":"258","notice":"Only the formal rounds (7:04–2:16:31), including openings, rebuttals, cross-examinations, and both closing statements, are assessed. Introductions, all later audience questions and answers, and the moderator's separate concluding argument are excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"sharpton-hitchens-god-religion-morality-2007","number":"261","notice":"This assessment uses the complete automatic English captions and audio verification of all 31 selected arguments across 23 passages. Under the primary-speaker exception, moderator Jacob Weisberg’s substantive civil-rights, deism, and scripture interventions, together with their directly dependent replies, are excluded from both speakers’ scores."},{"id":"dembski-hitchens-good-god-existence-2010","number":"263","notice":"This assessment uses the complete automatic captions and 13 bounded, independent caption-blind audio transcription checks. It involved no direct listening and no complete-audio verification. Quotations reproduce exact eligible transcript spans; argument descriptions and critiques are condensed from the source. Advocacy by Dan Panetti and Caleb, the unnamed moderator, and Larry Taylor was excluded, totaling about 4 minutes 58 seconds, or 3.45% of the assessed window. Neither side received credit or penalty for excluded participation or for replies dependent on it."},{"id":"chopra-dawkins-consciousness-purpose-spirituality-2013","number":"264","notice":"Direct quotations reproduce exact eligible substrings from the retained timestamped English captions; argument descriptions are condensed summaries of those captions. The retained scope was reviewed through the complete English caption record. Limited independent audio transcription covered 15 bounded verification clips and two source-context clips. No direct listening or complete-recording audio verification is claimed, and the clips establish wording and attribution rather than scientific or historical truth. This assessment covers the independent exchanges between Deepak Chopra and Richard Dawkins. By explicit editorial approval, Andrés Roemer’s substantive interventions and their directly dependent replies are excluded, including the moderator-led persuasion and free-will exchange. Neither debater receives credit or penalty for the excluded material; this is not an assessment of every contribution in the recording."},{"id":"dillahunty-giunta-faith-rational-justification-2018","number":"266","notice":"By explicit editorial approval, this assessment covers only the main conversation, approximately 05:29–1:16:07, not the full 2:28:59 recording or its audience question period. Both speakers agree that faith understood as confidence can be rational; the scored question concerns their narrower disagreement over whether God’s possibility must first be demonstrated. The moderator’s substantive interventions and their directly dependent replies, including the closing exchange from approximately 1:06:18, are excluded under the primary-speaker scope rule. Neither debater receives credit or penalty for excluded material. Complete English captions were reviewed for source screening; limited independent audio transcription checked the scope boundaries and speaker changes. No direct listening or complete-recording audio verification is claimed."},{"id":"hitchens-wilson-truth-goodness-beauty-2008","number":"267","notice":"This assessment covers only the opening presentations and direct exchange, approximately 11:46–1:13:22, with the final complete reply included. Audience Q&A, the later closing statements, and the introduction are not assessed. A verified replay at approximately 59:26–1:09:10 is counted only once; timestamps refer to the original upload. Scores describe this limited excerpt, not the complete event."},{"id":"sacks-dawkins-science-religion-2012","number":"268","notice":"This assessment covers selected, self-contained Jonathan Sacks–Richard Dawkins exchanges within the main conversation, approximately 2:09–41:54. Substantive moderator interventions and their dependent replies are excluded, removing approximately 20:31 (51.6%) of that window and leaving approximately 19:14 of eligible conversation. The exclusions include host-led exchanges about book tone, selective evidence, divine intervention, religious upbringing, antisemitism, and moral necessity. Introductions and all audience Q&A are also unscored. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material; the scores do not assess the complete event."},{"id":"craig-pigliucci-christian-god-2001","number":"271","notice":"This assessment covers both opening statements, both rounds of rebuttal, and both closing statements (approximately 6:31–1:39:05). The moderator’s subsequent third-position interlude and the entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers, are excluded. Neither side receives credit or penalty for excluded material; these scores assess the approved formal debate, not the complete event."},{"id":"craig-dacey-god-existence-2004","number":"273","notice":"This assessment covers both opening statements, both rounds of rebuttal, and both closing statements (approximately 5:00–1:40:07). The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, is excluded. Neither side receives credit or penalty for excluded material; these scores assess the approved formal debate, not the complete event."},{"id":"shermer-scrivener-good-without-god-2024","number":"277","notice":"By explicit editorial approval, this assessment scores only Michael Shermer and Glen Scrivener across the complete substantive exchange. Moderator Vince Vitale supplies substantive philosophical questions and challenges beyond the ordinary incidental-host allowance; his contributions remain context, and the debaters’ own replies remain eligible for assessment. No argument or score is assigned to a debater merely because Vitale advances it. Unequal questioning is considered when selecting moves and evaluating opportunities to respond. This is a recording-specific exception, not an assessment of a strictly two-speaker recording."},{"id":"lennox-tooley-atheism-christianity-2013","number":"278","notice":"The available recording ends after Michael Tooley’s closing remarks. A contemporary account also describes a closing statement by John Lennox, which is absent from the versions located. This assessment covers the available recording; neither speaker is penalized for material or replies missing from it."},{"id":"slick-barker-god-existence-2014","number":"279","notice":"This assessment uses the recoverable portions of the recording. A 33-second passage at the start of Matt Slick’s rebuttal (41:07.52–41:40.64) could not be reliably transcribed and is excluded. Neither speaker receives credit or a penalty for that passage, and no claim that an argument went unanswered relies on the missing material."},{"id":"turek-normark-god-existence-2014","number":"280","notice":"By explicit editorial approval, this assessment scores only Frank Turek and Dennis Nørmark across the substantive debate. Moderator Adam Holm contributes independent arguments and challenges, which remain context; the debaters’ own replies remain eligible for assessment. No moderator argument is credited to either debater. Unequal questioning is considered when selecting moves and evaluating opportunities to respond. This is a recording-specific exception to the ordinary two-speaker source rule."},{"id":"butt-barker-biblical-god-2009","number":"285","notice":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, and cross-examination (approximately 5:54–1:08:57), plus both closing statements (1:46:09–1:57:06). The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, is excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds and closings, not the complete event."},{"id":"nesan-dillahunty-jesus-prophecy-2021","number":"286","notice":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, cross-examination, and the speakers’ subsequent discussion before audience Q&A (approximately 0:12–1:02:31). This recording has no separate formal closing statements. The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, plus the host’s after-show, is excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the debate portion, not the complete recording."},{"id":"johnson-barker-god-existence-2022","number":"287","notice":"This assessment covers both formal opening statements and rebuttals (approximately 10:28–1:03:56), plus both closing statements (1:22:28–1:30:03). The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, and the hosts’ introductory and concluding discussion are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds and closings, not the complete recording."},{"id":"shermer-vandeweghe-jesus-miracles-2019","number":"288","notice":"This assessment covers both formal opening statements and rebuttals (approximately 6:56–1:11:59), plus both closing statements (1:49:07–1:58:23). The entire moderated discussion and audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, and the introductory montage and surrounding host remarks are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds and closings, not the complete recording."},{"id":"wielenberg-johnson-objective-morality-2020","number":"289","notice":"This assessment covers only the debaters’ direct exchange (approximately 33:05–1:13:25), as approved by the editor. The introductory position discussion, audience questions and all dependent answers, including the final reply to an audience question, are excluded. This recording has no separate formal opening speeches or paired closing statements. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the direct exchange, not the complete recording."},{"id":"rauser-malpass-evidential-problem-evil-2021","number":"290","notice":"This assessment covers Alex Malpass’s opening and the debaters’ direct exchange (approximately 6:33–1:06:47), as approved by the editor. Host introductions and interruptions, audience questions and every dependent answer, and the host-only remainder are excluded. This recording has no paired formal rounds or closing speeches. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the approved opening and exchange, not the complete recording."},{"id":"rauser-schieber-god-existence-2015","number":"291","notice":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, scheduled cross-examination and both closing statements (approximately 2:10–1:27:00). Moderator introductions and logistics, the entire audience Q&A with every dependent answer and reply, and concluding host material are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds, not the complete recording."},{"id":"thomason-clifton-morality-meaning-god-2020","number":"292","notice":"This was an informal conversation, with no formal opening rounds or closing speeches. At the editor’s request, this assessment covers only the direct philosophical exchanges at approximately 7:17–19:11, 22:27–36:37, and 1:02:32–1:15:37. Audience questions and all dependent answers and follow-ups, personal and political digressions, introductions and the outro are excluded. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the selected exchanges, not the complete recording."}],"year":"2020","videoId":"ycLfV4RVv5U","modelLabel":"5.6 Sol","tagCount":3,"people":[{"name":"S.J. Thomason","slug":"s-j-thomason"},{"name":"Scott Clifton","slug":"scott-clifton"}],"graph":{"sectionSideScores":3218,"minimum":48,"maximum":95,"buckets":[{"range":"48–49","count":1},{"range":"50–51","count":1},{"range":"52–53","count":1},{"range":"54–55","count":1},{"range":"56–57","count":3},{"range":"58–59","count":9},{"range":"60–61","count":10},{"range":"62–63","count":14},{"range":"64–65","count":37},{"range":"66–67","count":54},{"range":"68–69","count":79},{"range":"70–71","count":136},{"range":"72–73","count":190},{"range":"74–75","count":200},{"range":"76–77","count":287},{"range":"78–79","count":347},{"range":"80–81","count":386},{"range":"82–83","count":404},{"range":"84–85","count":339},{"range":"86–87","count":291},{"range":"88–89","